/*!
@page guides Guides

The guides in this section are step-by-step procedures to accomplish
common tasks with libbabeltrace2.

Guides help you navigate through the most important features of
the library and its API.

Make sure to eventually read \ref api-fund before you use the \bt_api
seriously.

See \ref examples for complete, working examples which apply the
concepts of some of the guides below.

The available guides are:

- \subpage guide-build-bt2-dev
- \subpage guide-comp-link-plugin-so
- \subpage guide-comp-link-plugin-provider-so
- \subpage guide-comp-link-app
- \subpage guide-mip-0-to-mip-1
- \subpage guide-create-graph
- \subpage guide-write-min-src-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-write-min-flt-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-write-simple-sink-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-create-plugin
- \subpage guide-write-full-src-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-write-full-flt-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-write-full-sink-comp-cls
- \subpage guide-query
- \subpage guide-seek-msg-iter
- \subpage guide-intr-graph
- \subpage guide-intr-query
- \subpage guide-graph-listeners

@page guide-build-bt2-dev Build Babeltrace 2 for development

If you're developing a \bt_name \bt_plugin or an application which uses
libbabeltrace2, then we recommend that:

- You build \bt_name from source in <em>developer mode</em>.

  The \bt_name developer mode enables more \ref api-fund-pre-post
  "precondition and postcondition" assertions to detect
  programming errors.

- You use \em TRACE as the minimal logging level at build time to have
  access to more \ref api-fund-logging "logging", should you need it
  to debug your plugin or application.

To build \bt_name from source in developer mode and using \em TRACE
as the minimal logging level:

<ol>
  <li>
    <a href="https://babeltrace.org/#bt2-get">Download the
    \bt_name tarball</a> and extract it.

    See the
    <a href="https://github.com/efficios/babeltrace/blob/stable-\bt_version_min_maj/README.adoc">README</a>
    of the project for build-time requirements and detailed build
    instructions.
  <li>
    Configure the build in developer mode and with the \em TRACE
    minimal logging level:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ BABELTRACE_DEV_MODE=1 BABELTRACE_MINIMAL_LOG_LEVEL=TRACE ./configure
    @endcode
  <li>
    Build and install the project:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ make
    # make install
    @endcode
</ol>

Other \bt_name developer mode build configuration example, disabling
the manual pages and the
\bt_man{babeltrace2-filter.lttng-utils.debug-info,7} component class:

@code{.unparsed}
$ BABELTRACE_DEV_MODE=1 BABELTRACE_MINIMAL_LOG_LEVEL=TRACE ./configure \
  --prefix="$PWD/install" --disable-man-pages --disable-debug-info
@endcode

@note
    @parblock
    The development build creates a libbabeltrace2 library which is
    slower to execute than a production (default) build.

    We believe that, during the development process, a less efficient,
    but more strict library is more desirable than the opposite.
    @endparblock

@page guide-comp-link-plugin-so Compile and link a Babeltrace 2 shared object plugin

To compile and link a
\link guide-create-plugin \bt_name shared object plugin\endlink:

<ol>
  <li>
    Compile the C/C++ source files of the plugin with the
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Code-Gen-Options.html"><code>\-fPIC</code></a>
    and
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Overall-Options.html"><code>\-c</code></a>
    compiler options to produce position-independent code and
    to compile without linking:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ cc my-plugin.c analysis.c -fPIC -c $(pkg-config --cflags babeltrace2)
    @endcode
  </li>

  <li>
    Link the resulting object files with the
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Link-Options.html"><code>\-shared</code></a>
    linker option and with the \bt_name library:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ ld my-plugin.o analysis.o -o my-plugin.so -shared $(pkg-config --libs babeltrace2)
    @endcode

    @note
        If you're using <a href="https://lld.llvm.org/">LLD</a>, then
        make sure to pass the <code>-z&nbsp;notext</code> option to
        the linker to allow relocations in read-only segments.
  </li>
</ol>

@note
    At least one of your C/C++ files must declare a \bt_name plugin
    and one or more \bt_p_comp_cls using the \ref api-plugin-so-dev macros.

@page guide-comp-link-plugin-provider-so Compile and link a Babeltrace 2 plugin provider

To compile and link a \bt_name plugin provider:

<ol>
  <li>
    Compile the C/C++ source files of the plugin provider with the
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Code-Gen-Options.html"><code>\-fPIC</code></a>
    and
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Overall-Options.html"><code>\-c</code></a>
    compiler options to produce position-independent code and
    to compile without linking:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ cc my-plugin-provider.c helper.c -fPIC -c $(pkg-config --cflags babeltrace2)
    @endcode
  </li>

  <li>
    Link the resulting object files with the
    <a href="https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Link-Options.html"><code>\-shared</code></a>
    linker option and with the \bt_name library:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ ld my-plugin-provider.o helper.o -o my-plugin-provider.so -shared $(pkg-config --libs babeltrace2)
    @endcode
  </li>
</ol>

@note
    At least one of your C/C++ files must declare a \bt_name plugin provider
    using the \ref api-plugin-provider-dev macros.

@page guide-comp-link-app Compile and link an application which uses libbabeltrace2

To compile and link an application which uses libbabeltrace2:

<ol>
  <li>
    Compile your C/C++ files as usual.
  </li>

  <li>
    Link the resulting object files with the \bt_name library:

    @code{.unparsed}
    $ ld my-app.o analysis.o -o my-app $(pkg-config --libs babeltrace2)
    @endcode
  </li>
</ol>

@page guide-mip-0-to-mip-1 Add MIP&nbsp;1 support to your component class

Babeltrace&nbsp;2.1 introduces new \ref api-tir "trace IR" features
under the new version&nbsp;1 of the
\ref api-msg-mip "Message Interchange Protocol" (MIP).

This guide shows the differences between MIP&nbsp;0 and MIP&nbsp;1
along with how to update your \ref api-comp-cls-dev "component class",
which already supports MIP&nbsp;0, to also support MIP&nbsp;1.

@section guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-impl-get-supported-mip-versions-meth Implement the "get supported MIP versions" method

Even though you could implement the
\link api-comp-cls-dev-meth-mip "get supported MIP versions"\endlink
component class method in Babeltrace&nbsp;2.0, you didn't have to
because without it, the default supported MIP version is&nbsp;0, which
was the only available one anyway.

Starting from Babeltrace&nbsp;2.1, your component may support
MIP&nbsp;0, MIP&nbsp;1, or both. It's now mandatory to implement the
"get supported MIP versions" method to support MIP&nbsp;1 as the
bt_graph_add_source_component_with_initialize_method_data(),
bt_graph_add_filter_component_with_initialize_method_data(), and
bt_graph_add_sink_component_with_initialize_method_data() functions
have the following precondition:

@pre
    The
    \link api-comp-cls-dev-meth-mip "get supported MIP versions"\endlink
    method of \bt_p{component_class}, given the same \bt_p{params},
    \bt_p{initialize_method_data}, and \bt_p{logging_level}
    parameters, makes one of its supported \bt_mip (MIP) version
    ranges contain the effective MIP version of \bt_p{graph} (see
    bt_graph_create()).

To make your component indicate MIP&nbsp;1 support:

-# In your \ref api-plugin-so-dev "plugin definition C&nbsp;file",
   use the BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD(),
   BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD(),
   or BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD()
   macro to set the "get supported MIP versions" method of your
   component class.

   For example:

   @code
   BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD(meow,
       meow_get_supported_mip_versions);
   @endcode

-# In your "get supported MIP versions" method, include version&nbsp;1
   in the supported version range.

   For example, to show support for both MIP&nbsp;0 and MIP&nbsp;1:

   @code
   bt_component_class_get_supported_mip_versions_method_status
   meow_get_supported_mip_versions(
       bt_self_component_class_sink * const self_component_class,
       const bt_value * const params, void * const initialize_method_data,
       const bt_logging_level logging_level,
       bt_integer_range_set_unsigned * const supported_versions)
   {
       if (bt_integer_range_set_unsigned_add_range(supported_versions, 0, 1) !=
                BT_INTEGER_RANGE_SET_ADD_RANGE_STATUS_OK) {
            return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD_STATUS_MEMORY_ERROR;
       }

       return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
   }
   @endcode

   You may rely on \bt_p{params} and \bt_p{initialize_method_data} to
   determine the exact MIP versions your component class instances
   expect to support.

@section guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-get-effective-mip-version Get the effective MIP version

Although most API functions work identically with both MIP&nbsp;0
and MIP&nbsp;1, some of them are only valid for a specific MIP version.

In a \bt_comp or \bt_msg_iter method, especially if your component
supports both available versions of the MIP, you might need to decide
which API function to use at run time depending on the current,
effective MIP version of the trace processing \bt_graph.

The available MIP version access functions are:

- bt_clock_class_get_graph_mip_version()
- bt_field_class_get_graph_mip_version()
- bt_self_component_get_graph_mip_version()
- bt_self_message_iterator_get_graph_mip_version()
- bt_trace_class_get_graph_mip_version()

For example, in a \bt_sink_comp printing the details of a \bt_trace,
you may only get its \ref api-tir-trace-prop-ns "namespace"
under MIP&nbsp;1:

@code
if (bt_self_component_get_graph_mip_version(self_component) == 1) {
    print_opt_string_property("Namespace", bt_trace_get_namespace(the_trace));
} else {
    print_none_property("Namespace");
}
@endcode

@section guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-changes Changes

MIP&nbsp;1 brings the required changes to support the features of
<a href="https://diamon.org/ctf/">CTF&nbsp;2</a>. As such:

- \bt_c_clock_cls, \bt_ev_cls, \bt_stream_cls, and \bt_trace
  objects may have a namespace, a name, and a
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unique_identifier">unique ID</a>
  (UID).

  The UUID property of clock class and trace objects is removed
  in favor of the new UID property.

- Important parts of the clock class API are changed.

- A \bt_ba_fc may have \ref api-tir-fc-ba-prop-flags "flags".

- An \bt_int_fc may have
  \ref api-tir-fc-int-prop-hints "field value hints".

- There are new \ref api-tir-fc-blob "BLOB field classes" and
  \ref api-tir-field-blob "fields".

- The \ref api-tir-field-loc "field location" system replaces the
  \ref api-tir-field-path "field path" one.

The following subsections provide details about the changes above
and how MIP&nbsp;0 translates to MIP&nbsp;1 in their context.

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-iden Namespace, name, and unique ID

The \bt_clock_cls, \bt_ev_cls, \bt_stream_cls, and \bt_trace
objects may have a namespace, a name, and a
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unique_identifier">unique ID</a>
(UID).

Together, we call those three optional properties the
\em identity of an object.

\bt_name doesn't specify what's the purpose of an identity and why
you should use it,
\ref api-tir-clock-cls-origin "except for clock classes": it exists
to accomodate trace formats.

On creation, an object has no identity (the values of the namespace,
name, and UID properties are missing).

For \ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-uuid "clock class" and
\ref api-tir-trace-prop-uuid "trace" objects, MIP&nbsp;1 removes the
UUID property in favor of those new properties. In other words,
you cannot use the following functions with MIP&nbsp;1:

- bt_clock_class_set_uuid()
- bt_clock_class_get_uuid()
- bt_trace_set_uuid()
- bt_trace_get_uuid()

A good strategy is to set the UID property to the
<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier#Textual_representation">textual representation</a>
of the UUID instead.

The new identity property access functions are:

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>\em Object
    <th>Namespace access
    <th>Name access (also valid with MIP&nbsp;0)
    <th>UID access
  <tr>
    <td><em>\ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-iden "Clock class"</em>
    <td>
      - bt_clock_class_set_namespace()
      - bt_clock_class_get_namespace()
    <td>
      - bt_clock_class_set_name()
      - bt_clock_class_get_name()
    <td>
      - bt_clock_class_set_uid()
      - bt_clock_class_get_uid()
  <tr>
    <td><em>\bt_c_ev_cls</em>
    <td>
      - bt_event_class_set_namespace()
      - bt_event_class_get_namespace()
    <td>
      - bt_event_class_set_name()
      - bt_event_class_get_name()
    <td>
      - bt_event_class_set_uid()
      - bt_event_class_get_uid()
  <tr>
    <td><em>\bt_c_stream_cls</em>
    <td>
      - bt_stream_class_set_namespace()
      - bt_stream_class_get_namespace()
    <td>
      - bt_stream_class_set_name()
      - bt_stream_class_get_name()
    <td>
      - bt_stream_class_set_uid()
      - bt_stream_class_get_uid()
  <tr>
    <td><em>\bt_c_trace</em>
    <td>
      - bt_trace_set_namespace()
      - bt_trace_get_namespace()
    <td>
      - bt_trace_set_name()
      - bt_trace_get_name()
    <td>
      - bt_trace_set_uid()
      - bt_trace_get_uid()
</table>

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-clock-cls-api Clock class API

The clock class API is changed as such from MIP&nbsp;0 to MIP&nbsp;1:

- The \ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-iden "identity" is
  the namespace, name, and UID tuple instead of the name and UUID pair.

  See the \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-iden section above.

- The \ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-precision "precision" property
  is optional.

  bt_clock_class_create() makes the initial precision unknown.

  Use the new bt_clock_class_get_opt_precision() function to get
  whether or not the precision is known and, if it's known, its value.

  bt_clock_class_get_precision() is not available and becomes
  deprecated.

- There's a new, optional
  \ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-precision "accuracy" property.

  The accuracy value is in clock cycles.

  While the precision property describes the random errors of clocks,
  the accuracy property describes systematic errors. From
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision">Accuracy
  and precision</a>:

  - Accuracy is how close a given set of measurements (observations or
    readings) are to their true value.

  - Precision is how close the measurements are to each other.

  bt_clock_class_create() makes the initial accuracy unknown.

  Use the new bt_clock_class_set_accuracy() and
  bt_clock_class_get_accuracy() functions.

  Having an unknown accuracy makes it possible to set the intended
  origin of a clock class while communicating that
  \ref api-tir-cs "clock snapshots" may not align with the true origin.

- The \ref api-tir-clock-cls-prop-origin "origin" property of a clock
  class, which is either unknown or the
  <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix_time">Unix epoch</a>
  with MIP&nbsp;0, can be custom.

  A custom origin is a point on the real timeline which is the base
  of clock snapshots. The Unix epoch is just a renowned origin with its
  own dedicated functions in \bt_name.

  Snapshots of clocks having the same origin, whatever the other clock
  class properties, have a correlation.

  A custom origin is identified by an optional namespace and a mandatory
  name/UID pair. Set the custom origin of a clock class with
  bt_clock_class_set_origin().

  Make the origin the Unix epoch with
  bt_clock_class_set_origin_unix_epoch() or unknown with
  bt_clock_class_set_origin_unknown().

  bt_clock_class_set_origin_is_unix_epoch() still works but is
  deprecated.

  The clock class origin property getters are:

  - bt_clock_class_get_origin_namespace()
  - bt_clock_class_get_origin_name()
  - bt_clock_class_get_origin_uid()
  - bt_clock_class_origin_is_known() (also valid with MIP&nbsp;0)
  - bt_clock_class_origin_is_unix_epoch() (also valid with MIP&nbsp;0)

  bt_clock_class_create() still sets the initial origin to the
  Unix epoch.

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-ba-fc-flags Bit array field class flags

A \bt_ba_fc may have zero or more \ref api-tir-fc-ba-prop-flags "flags".

The bit array field class flag API is very similar to the
\ref api-tir-fc-enum-prop-mappings "unsigned enumeration field class mapping"
API.

A flag assigns a label to one or more bits, by indexes, of instances
(\bt_p_ba_field). When \em any of the bits of some flag is set (true)
within a bit array field, said flag is known to be <em>active</em>.

The new related functions are:

<dl>
  <dt>Add a flag to a bit array field class</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_bit_array_add_flag()</dd>

  <dt>Get the number of flags in a bit array field class</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_bit_array_get_flag_count()</dd>

  <dt>Borrow a flag from a bit array field class</dt>
  <dd>
    - bt_field_class_bit_array_borrow_flag_by_index_const()
    - bt_field_class_bit_array_borrow_flag_by_label_const()
  </dd>

  <dt>Get the label of a bit array field class flag</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_bit_array_flag_get_label()</dd>

  <dt>Borrow the bit index range set of a bit array field class flag</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_bit_array_flag_borrow_index_ranges_const()</dd>

  <dt>
    Get the labels of all the active flags of a bit array field class
    for the set bits of some integral value
  </dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_bit_array_get_active_flag_labels_for_value_as_integer()</dd>

  <dt>
    Get the labels of all the active flags of the class of a bit array
    field for the set bits of its integral value
  </dt>
  <dd>bt_field_bit_array_get_active_flag_labels()</dd>
</dl>

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-int-fc-hints Integer field class field value hints

An \bt_int_fc may have
\ref api-tir-fc-int-prop-hints "field value hints".

An integer field class field value hint is a marker that provides
additional context about the expected characteristics of instance
(integer field) values. These hints serve as non-binding recommendations
to guide downstream components about specific properties or
optimizations.

As of \bt_name_version_min_maj, the only available hint is
#BT_FIELD_CLASS_INTEGER_FIELD_VALUE_HINT_SMALL which means that
instances of the integer field class are expected to hold small integer
values.

The new related functions are:

<dl>
  <dt>Set the field value hints of an integer field class</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_integer_set_field_value_hints()</dd>

  <dt>Get the field value hints of an integer field class</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_integer_get_field_value_hints()</dd>

  <dt>Get whether or not an integer field class has a given field value hint</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_class_integer_has_field_value_hint()</dd>
</dl>

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-blob BLOB field classes and fields

A \bt_blob_fc describes \bt_p_blob_field which contain zero or more
bytes of binary data.

With MIP&nbsp;0, you can use a \bt_string_fc or an \bt_array_fc with an
8-bit \ref api-tir-fc-int "unsigned integer element field class" as
the class of some byte container. However, both have their limitations.

A BLOB field is the perfect container of pure binary data.

Like static and dynamic \bt_p_array_fc, you can create both static and
dynamic BLOB field classes:

<dl>
  <dt>bt_field_class_blob_static_create()</dt>
  <dd>
    Is a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_BLOB and
    a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_STATIC_BLOB.
  </dd>

  <dt>bt_field_class_blob_dynamic_without_length_field_location_create()</dt>
  <dd>
    Is a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_BLOB,
    a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_DYNAMIC_BLOB,
    and a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_DYNAMIC_BLOB_WITHOUT_LENGTH_FIELD.
  </dd>

  <dt>bt_field_class_blob_dynamic_with_length_field_location_create()</dt>
  <dd>
    Is a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_BLOB,
    a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_DYNAMIC_BLOB,
    and a #BT_FIELD_CLASS_TYPE_DYNAMIC_BLOB_WITH_LENGTH_FIELD.

    This one accepts a \bt_field_loc: see the
    \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-field-loc section
    below to learn more.
  </dd>
</dl>

A BLOB field class has a
\ref api-tir-fc-blob-prop-media-type "media type" property which the
creation functions initialize to <code>application/octet-stream</code>,
a very generic one. The media type indicates which kind of data the
instances hold.

Just like with a \bt_darray_field, set the length of a dynamic BLOB
field with bt_field_blob_dynamic_set_length() before getting its
internal buffer.

Get the length of a BLOB field with bt_field_blob_get_length().

Get the internal buffer of a BLOB field with:

<dl>
  <dt>Write mode</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_blob_get_data()</dd>

  <dt>Read mode</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_blob_get_data_const()</dd>
</dl>

@subsection guide-mip-0-to-mip-1-field-loc Field location API

Starting from MIP&nbsp;1, the classes of all the
\ref api-tir-fc-link "fields with links to other fields" use a
\bt_field_loc to show the link instead of a \bt_field_path.

A field location is different from a field path: it contains a
list of \bt_struct_field member <em>names</em> to follow from some root
scope to reach the target field. This makes it possible, for example,
for the length field of some dynamic field to be located within one or
more distant \bt_p_opt_field and/or \bt_p_var_field.

See \ref api-tir-field-loc-proc "Field location procedure" to learn
the exact procedure to locate a field.

Whereas libbabeltrace2 creates field path objects for you when you
provide the target field class at dependent field class creation time,
you create a field location object yourself. This is because a dependent
field class may target more than one field class.

The field location API is:

<dl>
  <dt>Create a field location</dt>
  <dd>
    bt_field_location_create()

    You provide the root scope and all the items (structure member
    names) to this function.
  </dd>

  <dt>Get the root scope of a field location</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_location_get_root_scope()</dd>

  <dt>Get the number of items in a field location</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_location_get_item_count()</dd>

  <dt>Get an item by index from a field location</dt>
  <dd>bt_field_location_get_item_by_index()</dd>

  <dt>Reference count</dt>
  <dd>
    - bt_field_location_get_ref()
    - bt_field_location_put_ref()
    - BT_FIELD_LOCATION_PUT_REF_AND_RESET()
    - BT_FIELD_LOCATION_MOVE_REF()
  </dd>
</dl>

The new field class creation functions to use are:

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>MIP&nbsp;0 function
    <th>MIP&nbsp;1 function
  <tr>
    <td>N/A
    <td>bt_field_class_blob_dynamic_without_length_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>N/A
    <td>bt_field_class_blob_dynamic_with_length_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>
      bt_field_class_array_dynamic_create() with the
      \bt_p{length_field_class} parameter set to \c NULL
    <td>bt_field_class_array_dynamic_without_length_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>
      bt_field_class_array_dynamic_create() with the
      \bt_p{length_field_class} parameter set to an \bt_uint_fc
    <td>bt_field_class_array_dynamic_with_length_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>bt_field_class_option_without_selector_create()
    <td>bt_field_class_option_without_selector_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_bool_create()
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_location_bool_create()
  <tr>
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_integer_unsigned_create()
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_location_integer_unsigned_create()
  <tr>
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_integer_signed_create()
    <td>bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_location_integer_signed_create()
  <tr>
    <td>
      bt_field_class_variant_create() with the
      \bt_p{selector_field_class} parameter set to \c NULL
    <td>bt_field_class_variant_without_selector_field_location_create()
  <tr>
    <td>
      bt_field_class_variant_create() with the
      \bt_p{selector_field_class} parameter set to an unsigned
      integer field class
    <td>bt_field_class_variant_with_selector_field_location_integer_unsigned_create()
  <tr>
    <td>
      bt_field_class_variant_create() with the
      \bt_p{selector_field_class} parameter set to a signed integer
      field class
    <td>bt_field_class_variant_with_selector_field_location_integer_signed_create()
</table>

The new field location borrowing functions are:

- bt_field_class_blob_dynamic_with_length_field_borrow_length_field_location_const()
- bt_field_class_array_dynamic_with_length_field_borrow_length_field_location_const()
- bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_borrow_selector_field_location_const()
- bt_field_class_variant_with_selector_field_borrow_selector_field_location_const()

The following functions aren't available with MIP&nbsp;1:

- All the \ref api-tir-field-path "field path API" functions
- bt_field_class_array_dynamic_create()
- bt_field_class_option_without_selector_create()
- bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_bool_create()
- bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_integer_unsigned_create()
- bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_integer_signed_create()
- bt_field_class_variant_create()
- bt_field_class_array_dynamic_with_length_field_borrow_length_field_path_const()
- bt_field_class_option_with_selector_field_borrow_selector_field_path_const()
- bt_field_class_variant_with_selector_field_borrow_selector_field_path_const()

@page guide-create-graph Create a trace processing graph from existing component classes and run it

This guide shows how to build a \bt_graph from existing \bt_p_comp_cls,
connect the \bt_p_port of its \bt_p_comp, and run it to accomplish a
trace processing task.

A component class can come from a \bt_plugin, in which case you first
need to find and load it (see \ref api-plugin), or be one you
\ref api-comp-cls-dev "create dynamically" within your application, for
example following the \ref guide-write-min-src-comp-cls "minimal" or
\ref guide-write-full-src-comp-cls "complete" component class guides.

As an example, this guide builds a graph which reads a
<a href="https://diamon.org/ctf/">CTF</a> trace from the file system
with the <code>source.ctf.fs</code> \bt_src_comp_cls of the \c ctf
\bt_plugin (see \bt_man{babeltrace2-source.ctf.fs,7}) and pretty-prints
its messages with the <code>sink.text.pretty</code> \bt_sink_comp_cls
of the \c text plugin (see \bt_man{babeltrace2-sink.text.pretty,7}).

@note
    @parblock
    To keep this example simple, it directly connects the first
    \bt_oport of the <code>source.ctf.fs</code> component, which
    corresponds to a single CTF data stream, to the single \bt_iport
    of the <code>sink.text.pretty</code> component.

    A CTF trace often contains more than one data stream, in which
    case the <code>source.ctf.fs</code> component creates one
    \bt_oport per stream. To merge and sort the messages of more
    than one \bt_oport before they reach a single \bt_sink_comp,
    connect them to the input ports of a
    <code>filter.utils.muxer</code> \bt_flt_comp instead
    (see \bt_man{babeltrace2-filter.utils.muxer,7}),
    and then connect its single output port to the sink component.
    @endparblock

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

To create a trace processing graph, add components to it, connect
their ports, and run it:

<ol>
  <li>
    Find and load the plugins which provide the \bt_p_comp_cls you
    want to add to the graph (see \ref api-plugin), and then borrow
    those component classes by name with
    bt_plugin_borrow_source_component_class_by_name_const() and
    bt_plugin_borrow_sink_component_class_by_name_const().

    @code
    const bt_plugin *ctf_plugin;

    bt_plugin_find("ctf", BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, &ctf_plugin);

    const bt_plugin *text_plugin;

    bt_plugin_find("text", BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, BT_TRUE, &text_plugin);

    const bt_component_class_source * const source_comp_class =
        bt_plugin_borrow_source_component_class_by_name_const(ctf_plugin, "fs");
    const bt_component_class_sink * const sink_comp_class =
        bt_plugin_borrow_sink_component_class_by_name_const(text_plugin, "pretty");
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create a default (empty) trace processing graph with
    bt_graph_create().

    You must pass the effective \bt_mip (MIP) version with which the
    graph operates. Compute it from the component classes you intend
    to add to the graph with
    bt_get_greatest_operative_mip_version(). See
    \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1 to learn about supporting multiple MIP
    versions. As an example, this guide assumes that MIP&nbsp;1 is the
    operative version:

    @code
    bt_graph * const graph = bt_graph_create(1);
    @endcode
  <li>
    \ref api-graph-lc-add "Add" one or more components to the graph.

    Instantiate a \bt_comp_cls with bt_graph_add_source_component(),
    bt_graph_add_filter_component(), or bt_graph_add_sink_component(),
    depending on its type, or with the corresponding
    <code>*_with_initialize_method_data()</code> variant if you need to
    pass custom data to the
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method" of the
    component class.

    Each function needs the graph, the component class to instantiate,
    a unique component name within the graph, initialization
    parameters, and an initial logging level. On success, it sets an
    output parameter to the created \bt_comp.

    The <code>source.ctf.fs</code> component class expects, as
    initialization parameters, a map value with an <code>inputs</code>
    entry: an array of paths to physical CTF trace directories to
    read. The <code>sink.text.pretty</code> component class doesn't
    need any initialization parameter.

    @code
    bt_value * const source_params = bt_value_map_create();
    bt_value *inputs;

    bt_value_map_insert_empty_array_entry(source_params, "inputs", &inputs);
    bt_value_array_append_string_element(inputs, "/path/to/my-ctf-trace");

    const bt_component_source *source_comp;

    bt_graph_add_source_component(graph, source_comp_class, "the-source", source_params,
                                  BT_LOGGING_LEVEL_WARNING, &source_comp);

    bt_value_put_ref(source_params);

    const bt_component_sink *sink_comp;

    bt_graph_add_sink_component(graph, sink_comp_class, "the-sink", NULL,
                                BT_LOGGING_LEVEL_WARNING, &sink_comp);
    @endcode

    Once you added the component classes you need to the graph, you
    can discard the plugins: the graph keeps their component classes
    valid, independently of the plugin from which you borrowed them.
    You need to add the component classes to the graph \em before you
    discard the plugins, as nothing else keeps a component class alive
    on its own.

    @code
    bt_plugin_put_ref(ctf_plugin);
    bt_plugin_put_ref(text_plugin);
    @endcode

    To add a very basic \bt_sink_comp without having to write and
    \ref guide-create-plugin "package" a whole sink component class,
    use bt_graph_add_simple_sink_component() instead: see
    \ref api-graph-lc-add-ss "Add a simple sink component".
  <li>
    \ref api-graph-lc-connect "Connect" the ports of the added
    components.

    Borrow an \bt_oport or an \bt_iport of a component by index or by
    name, for example with
    bt_component_source_borrow_output_port_by_index_const() or
    bt_component_sink_borrow_input_port_by_name_const(), and then
    connect an output port to an input port with
    bt_graph_connect_ports().

    For this example, connect the first \bt_oport, at index&nbsp;0, of
    the <code>source.ctf.fs</code> component directly to the single
    \bt_iport of the <code>sink.text.pretty</code> component:

    @code
    bt_graph_connect_ports(graph,
                           bt_component_source_borrow_output_port_by_index_const(source_comp, 0),
                           bt_component_sink_borrow_input_port_by_index_const(sink_comp, 0),
                           NULL);
    @endcode

    You can repeat steps&nbsp;3 and&nbsp;4, in any order, until the
    graph has the topology you need.

    Connecting a port can make its
    component add new ports in reaction, for example within its
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-iport-connected "\"input port connected\""
    or \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-oport-connected "output port
    connected" method: see \ref api-graph-listeners "Listeners" to
    learn how to react to such topology changes yourself, for example
    to connect those new ports automatically
    (\ref guide-graph-listeners).
  <li>
    \ref api-graph-lc-run "Run" the graph with bt_graph_run().

    @code
    const bt_graph_run_status status = bt_graph_run(graph);
    @endcode

    bt_graph_run() doesn't return until all the \bt_p_sink_comp of the
    graph are done, one of them returns an error, or the graph is
    interrupted (see \ref guide-intr-graph).

    If bt_graph_run() returns #BT_GRAPH_RUN_STATUS_AGAIN, then one of
    the sink components needs you to try again later: call
    bt_graph_run() again, usually after waiting for some time.

    To only make a single sink component consume and process a few
    messages before controlling the thread again, for example to
    integrate the graph within an existing event loop, use
    bt_graph_run_once() instead.
  <li>
    Clean up.

    At this point, the graph is the only \ref api-fund-shared-object
    "shared object" reference you still own: the component classes
    and the  components you borrowed above aren't owned
    references, and you already discarded the plugin and
    initialization parameter references.

    Once you're done with the
    graph, put its reference with bt_graph_put_ref():

    @code
    bt_graph_put_ref(graph);
    @endcode
</ol>

@note
    If \em any of the functions above returns an error status code
    (ending with <code>ERROR</code>), then the trace processing graph
    becomes <strong>faulty</strong>: you cannot use it anymore, except
    to put its reference with bt_graph_put_ref().

@page guide-write-min-src-comp-cls Write a minimal source component class

This guide shows how to write the smallest possible \bt_src_comp_cls:
one which compiles, which you can instantiate and connect within a
\bt_graph (see \ref guide-create-graph), but which doesn't produce any
\bt_p_msg.

See \ref guide-write-full-src-comp-cls to learn how to implement all
the optional methods of a source component class on top of this
minimal skeleton.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

To write a minimal source component class:

<ol>
  <li>
    Implement the mandatory
    \link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method of its
    \bt_msg_iter_cls.

    A source component class doesn't produce \bt_p_msg itself: it's
    a \bt_msg_iter, instantiated from its \bt_msg_iter_cls for one
    of the \bt_p_oport of a component instance, which does.

    The minimal "next" method doesn't fill the received message array
    at all and immediately indicates the end of the message iterator
    sequence:

    @code
    static bt_message_iterator_class_next_method_status
    my_src_iter_next(bt_self_message_iterator * const self_msg_iter,
                     const bt_message_array_const messages,
                     const uint64_t capacity, uint64_t * const count)
    {
        return BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_NEXT_METHOD_STATUS_END;
    }
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create the \bt_msg_iter_cls with bt_message_iterator_class_create(),
    passing the "next" method above.

    @code
    bt_message_iterator_class * const msg_iter_class =
        bt_message_iterator_class_create(my_src_iter_next);
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create the source component class with
    bt_component_class_source_create(), passing a name and the message
    iterator class above.

    @code
    bt_component_class_source * const comp_class =
        bt_component_class_source_create("my-source", msg_iter_class);
    @endcode
  <li>
    Implement an \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method"
    which adds a single \bt_oport to the component.

    Without at least one output port, no \bt_comp instance of this
    component class could ever be
    \ref api-graph-lc-connect "connected" within a trace processing
    graph, which makes it useless: the initialization method is
    therefore the only non-optional part of this guide beyond the
    "next" method above.

    @code
    static bt_component_class_initialize_method_status
    my_src_init(bt_self_component_source * const self_comp,
                bt_self_component_source_configuration * const config,
                const bt_value * const params, void * const init_method_data)
    {
        bt_self_component_source_add_output_port(self_comp, "out", NULL, NULL);
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set it with bt_component_class_source_set_initialize_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD() macro
    in a plugin definition file:

    @code
    bt_component_class_source_set_initialize_method(comp_class, my_src_init);
    @endcode
</ol>

You can now \ref guide-create-plugin "package" \bt_p{comp_class}
within a \bt_plugin, or add it directly to a trace processing graph
if you're creating it dynamically within your application (see
\ref guide-create-graph).

@page guide-write-min-flt-comp-cls Write a minimal filter component class

This guide shows how to write the smallest possible \bt_flt_comp_cls:
one which compiles, which you can instantiate and connect within a
\bt_graph (see \ref guide-create-graph), but which doesn't produce any
\bt_p_msg.

This guide is very similar to \ref guide-write-min-src-comp-cls
"Write a minimal source component class": the only differences are the
component class creation function to call and the fact that a
\bt_flt_comp needs both an \bt_iport and a \bt_oport to be useful.

See \ref guide-write-full-flt-comp-cls to learn how to implement all
the optional methods of a filter component class on top of this
minimal skeleton.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

To write a minimal filter component class:

<ol>
  <li>
    Implement the mandatory
    \link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method of its
    \bt_msg_iter_cls.

    The minimal "next" method doesn't fill the received message array
    at all and immediately indicates the end of the message iterator
    sequence, without even creating an upstream message iterator to
    filter from:

    @code
    static bt_message_iterator_class_next_method_status
    my_flt_iter_next(bt_self_message_iterator * const self_msg_iter,
                     const bt_message_array_const messages,
                     const uint64_t capacity, uint64_t * const count)
    {
        return BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_NEXT_METHOD_STATUS_END;
    }
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create the \bt_msg_iter_cls with bt_message_iterator_class_create(),
    passing the "next" method above.

    @code
    bt_message_iterator_class * const msg_iter_class =
        bt_message_iterator_class_create(my_flt_iter_next);
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create the filter component class with
    bt_component_class_filter_create(), passing a name and the message
    iterator class above.

    @code
    bt_component_class_filter * const comp_class =
        bt_component_class_filter_create("my-filter", msg_iter_class);
    @endcode
  <li>
    Implement an \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method"
    which adds a single \bt_iport and a single \bt_oport to the
    component.

    Without at least one input port and one output port, no \bt_comp
    instance of this component class could ever be
    \ref api-graph-lc-connect "connected" within a trace processing
    graph, which makes it useless.

    @code
    static bt_component_class_initialize_method_status
    my_flt_init(bt_self_component_filter * const self_comp,
                bt_self_component_filter_configuration * const config,
                const bt_value * const params, void * const init_method_data)
    {
        bt_self_component_filter_add_input_port(self_comp, "in", NULL, NULL);
        bt_self_component_filter_add_output_port(self_comp, "out", NULL, NULL);
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set it with bt_component_class_filter_set_initialize_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD() macro
    in a plugin definition file:

    @code
    bt_component_class_filter_set_initialize_method(comp_class, my_flt_init);
    @endcode
</ol>

You can now \ref guide-create-plugin "package" \bt_p{component_class}
within a \bt_plugin, or add it directly to a trace processing graph
if you're creating it dynamically within your application (see
\ref guide-create-graph).

@page guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls Write a minimal sink component class

This guide shows how to write the smallest possible \bt_sink_comp_cls:
one which compiles, and which you can instantiate and connect within a
\bt_graph (see \ref guide-create-graph), but which doesn't consume or
process any \bt_p_msg.

See \ref guide-write-simple-sink-comp-cls to learn how to make a
minimal sink component class like this one actually consume messages,
and \ref guide-write-full-sink-comp-cls to learn how to implement all
the optional methods of a sink component class.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

To write a minimal sink component class:

<ol>
  <li>
    Implement the mandatory
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume "consuming" method.

    The minimal consuming method doesn't get or process any message: it
    immediately indicates that the component is done:

    @code
    static bt_component_class_sink_consume_method_status
    my_sink_consume(bt_self_component_sink * const self_comp)
    {
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_END;
    }
    @endcode
  <li>
    Create the sink component class with
    bt_component_class_sink_create(), passing a name and the consuming
    method above.

    @code
    bt_component_class_sink * const comp_class =
        bt_component_class_sink_create("my-sink", my_sink_consume);
    @endcode
  <li>
    Implement an \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method"
    which adds a single \bt_iport to the component.

    Without at least one input port, no \bt_comp instance of this
    component class could ever be
    \ref api-graph-lc-connect "connected" within a trace processing
    graph, which makes it useless.

    @code
    static bt_component_class_initialize_method_status
    my_sink_init(bt_self_component_sink * const self_comp,
                 bt_self_component_sink_configuration * const config,
                 const bt_value * const params, void * const init_method_data)
    {
        bt_self_component_sink_add_input_port(self_comp, "in", NULL, NULL);
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set it with bt_component_class_sink_set_initialize_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD() macro
    in a plugin definition file:

    @code
    bt_component_class_sink_set_initialize_method(component_class, my_sink_init);
    @endcode
</ol>

You can now \ref guide-create-plugin "package" \bt_p{component_class}
within a \bt_plugin, or add it directly to a trace processing graph
if you're creating it dynamically within your application (see
\ref guide-create-graph).

@page guide-write-simple-sink-comp-cls Write a simple sink component class

This guide shows how to make a \ref guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls
"minimal sink component class" actually consume and process
\bt_p_msg, which is the whole point of writing a \bt_sink_comp_cls.

For a complete, working example based on the steps below, see
\ref example-simple-sink-cmp-cls.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

Starting from a minimal sink component class which has a single
\bt_iport named <code>in</code> (see
\ref guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls):

<ol>
  <li>
    Implement a \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-graph-configured
    "\"graph is configured\"" method which creates a \bt_msg_iter on
    the input port of the component.

    This method is called once, \em after the
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method", and it's
    the earliest point at which you can create a message iterator: at
    initialization time, the ports of other components aren't
    necessarily connected yet.

    Borrow the input port with
    bt_self_component_sink_borrow_input_port_by_index() or
    bt_self_component_sink_borrow_input_port_by_name(), then create the
    message iterator with
    bt_message_iterator_create_from_sink_component().

    Keep the created \bt_msg_iter somewhere you can find it again from
    the consuming method below, for example in a private structure that
    you set as the user data of the \bt_comp with
    bt_self_component_set_data().

    @code
    static bt_component_class_sink_graph_is_configured_method_status
    my_sink_graph_is_configured(bt_self_component_sink * const self_comp)
    {
        struct my_sink_data * const data = bt_self_component_get_data(
            bt_self_component_sink_as_self_component(self_comp));

        bt_message_iterator_create_from_sink_component(self_comp,
            bt_self_component_sink_borrow_input_port_by_index(self_comp, 0),
            &data->msg_iter);

        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_GRAPH_IS_CONFIGURED_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set it with bt_component_class_sink_set_graph_is_configured_method(),
    or with the BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_GRAPH_IS_CONFIGURED_METHOD()
    macro in a \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  <li>
    Rewrite the \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume "consuming" method to
    get a batch of messages from the message iterator with
    bt_message_iterator_next() and process each of them.

    @code
    static bt_component_class_sink_consume_method_status
    my_sink_consume(bt_self_component_sink * const self_comp)
    {
        struct my_sink_data * const data = bt_self_component_get_data(
            bt_self_component_sink_as_self_component(self_comp));
        bt_message_array_const messages;
        uint64_t msg_count;
        bt_component_class_sink_consume_method_status status =
            BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
        const bt_message_iterator_next_status next_status =
            bt_message_iterator_next(data->msg_iter, &messages, &msg_count);

        if (next_status != BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_NEXT_STATUS_OK) {
            /* Map the message iterator status to a consuming status */
            status = (int) next_status;
            goto end;
        }

        for (uint64_t i = 0; i < msg_count; i++) {
            /* Do something with `messages[i]` here */
            bt_message_put_ref(messages[i]);
        }

    end:
        return status;
    }
    @endcode

    Mind the following message iterator statuses:

    <dl>
      <dt>#BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_NEXT_STATUS_END</dt>
      <dd>
        Put the message iterator reference with
        bt_message_iterator_put_ref() and return
        #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_END.
      </dd>

      <dt>#BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_NEXT_STATUS_AGAIN</dt>
      <dd>
        Return #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN.
      </dd>
    </dl>
  <li>
    Implement a \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-fini "finalization" method
    which frees the private data of the component, putting the
    reference of the message iterator first, if it's not already put
    (that is, if the consuming method above didn't reach the end of the
    message iterator sequence).

    Set it with bt_component_class_sink_set_finalize_method(), or with
    the BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD() macro in a
    plugin definition file.
</ol>

@page guide-create-plugin Create a Babeltrace 2 plugin

This guide shows how to package one or more \bt_p_comp_cls, for
example ones you wrote by following the
\ref guide-write-min-src-comp-cls "component class writing guides",
as a \bt_name \ref api-plugin-so-dev "shared object plugin".

For a complete, working example based on the steps below, see
\ref example-simple-plugin-def-file.

To create a shared object plugin, write a
<em>plugin definition C file</em>:

<ol>
  <li>
    Start the file with:

    @code
    BT_PLUGIN_MODULE();
    @endcode
  <li>
    Define the plugin itself with BT_PLUGIN(), passing its name:

    @code
    BT_PLUGIN(my_plugin);
    @endcode

    The name of a plugin doesn't need to be a valid C identifier if you
    use BT_PLUGIN_WITH_ID() instead: see
    \link api-plugin-so-dev-custom-plugin-id "Custom plugin ID"\endlink
    to learn more.
  <li>
    \bt_dt_opt Set plugin properties with BT_PLUGIN_AUTHOR(),
    BT_PLUGIN_DESCRIPTION(), BT_PLUGIN_LICENSE(), and
    BT_PLUGIN_VERSION():

    @code
    BT_PLUGIN_DESCRIPTION("Reads and writes the Tamebsz format.");
    BT_PLUGIN_AUTHOR("Klek de Poitrine");
    BT_PLUGIN_LICENSE("MIT");
    @endcode
  <li>
    \bt_dt_opt Set the initialization and finalization functions of the
    plugin with BT_PLUGIN_INITIALIZE_FUNC() and
    BT_PLUGIN_FINALIZE_FUNC().

    The library calls the initialization function when it loads the
    shared object (see \ref api-plugin-loading), and the finalization
    function when the resulting \bt_plugin object is destroyed.
  <li>
    Add each component class of the plugin with
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS(),
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS(), or
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS(), depending on its type, passing
    the name of the component class and its mandatory method (a
    \bt_msg_iter_cls "next" method for a source or filter component
    class, or a consuming method for a sink component class):

    @code
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS(input, my_src_iter_next);
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS(output, my_sink_consume);
    @endcode
  <li>
    \bt_dt_opt For each component class of step&nbsp;5, set its other
    properties and optional methods with the
    <code>BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_*()</code>,
    <code>BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_*()</code>, and
    <code>BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_*()</code> macros, for example:

    @code
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_DESCRIPTION(input, "Read a Tamebsz file.");
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD(input, my_src_initialize);
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD(input, my_src_finalize);
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD(output, my_sink_initialize);
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_GRAPH_IS_CONFIGURED_METHOD(output,
                                                              my_sink_graph_is_configured);
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD(output, my_sink_finalize);
    @endcode

    See \ref api-plugin-so-dev for the complete list of available
    macros, one per optional
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-methods "component class method" and
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-methods "message iterator class method".
</ol>

The component class methods themselves (<code>my_src_iter_next</code>,
<code>my_src_initialize</code>, <code>my_sink_consume</code>, and so on
above) can be defined in the same C file or in other C files of your
project: the <code>BT_PLUGIN_*()</code> macros only need their
declarations, found, for example, in a common header file.

Once your plugin definition C file (and any other C file it depends
on) is ready, \ref guide-comp-link-plugin-so "compile and link it" as a
shared object plugin.

@page guide-write-full-src-comp-cls Write a complete source component class

This guide lists all the optional methods you can add to a
\ref guide-write-min-src-comp-cls "minimal source component class" to
make it robust and complete, ready for production use.

For a working example which implements some of the methods below, see
\ref example-simple-src-cmp-cls.

@section guide-write-full-src-comp-cls-comp-cls-methods Component class methods

On top of the mandatory \bt_msg_iter_cls
\link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method and the
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method" of a
\ref guide-write-min-src-comp-cls "minimal source component class",
consider implementing:

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-fini "Finalize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Destroy, free, or otherwise finalize any user data you attached to
    the component with bt_self_component_set_data() in the
    initialization method.

    Set it with bt_component_class_source_set_finalize_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD() macro
    in a
    \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-mip "Get supported MIP versions"</dt>
  <dd>
    Implement this \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method" if
    your component class supports \bt_mip (MIP)&nbsp;1: without it,
    the library assumes that any instance of your component class only
    supports MIP&nbsp;version&nbsp;0.

    See \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1 to learn how to support both MIP
    versions.

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_source_set_get_supported_mip_versions_method(),
    or with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-oport-connected "Output port connected"</dt>
  <dd>
    React to one of the \bt_p_oport of the component becoming
    connected, for example to add more output ports depending on the
    \bt_comp at the other end of the new \bt_conn (see
    \ref guide-graph-listeners for another way to react to topology
    changes from outside the component).

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_source_set_output_port_connected_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_OUTPUT_PORT_CONNECTED_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "Query"</dt>
  <dd>
    Let users of your component class, before or without instantiating
    it, ask arbitrary questions about it, for example to list the
    compatible traces that a given path contains.

    See \ref guide-query to learn how to implement and call this
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method".

    Set it with bt_component_class_source_set_query_method(), or with
    the BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD() macro in a
    plugin definition file.
  </dd>
</dl>

@section guide-write-full-src-comp-cls-msg-iter-cls-methods Message iterator class methods

On top of the mandatory
\link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method, consider
implementing:

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-init "Initialize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Perform any setup your \bt_msg_iter needs before its "next" method
    is called for the first time, and attach user data to it with
    bt_self_message_iterator_set_data().

    Set it with bt_message_iterator_class_set_initialize_method(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD()
    macro in a
    \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-fini "Finalize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Destroy, free, or otherwise finalize any user data you attached to
    the message iterator in its initialization method.

    Set it with bt_message_iterator_class_set_finalize_method(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-beg "Can seek beginning?"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-beg "Seek beginning"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-ns "Can seek ns from origin?"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-ns "Seek ns from origin"
  </dt>
  <dd>
    Make your message iterator able to seek its beginning or a specific
    point in time.

    See \ref guide-seek-msg-iter to learn how to implement those
    methods.

    Set them with
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_beginning_methods() and
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_ns_from_origin_methods(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_BEGINNING_METHODS()
    and
    BT_PLUGIN_SOURCE_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_NS_FROM_ORIGIN_METHODS()
    macros in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>
</dl>

Whether you implement all, some, or none of the methods above depends
entirely on the trace processing task your source component class
needs to accomplish. See the existing \bt_name \bt_p_plugin
for real-world examples of source component classes.

@page guide-write-full-flt-comp-cls Write a complete filter component class

This guide lists all the optional methods you can add to a
\ref guide-write-min-flt-comp-cls "minimal filter component class" to
make it robust and complete, ready for production use.

This guide is very similar to
\ref guide-write-full-src-comp-cls "Write a complete source component class":
the only differences are
the component class type and the fact that a filter component class
can react to both its \bt_p_iport and its \bt_p_oport becoming
connected.

For a working example which implements some of the methods below, see
\ref example-simple-flt-cmp-cls.

@section guide-write-full-flt-comp-cls-comp-cls-methods Component class methods

On top of the mandatory \bt_msg_iter_cls
\link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method and the
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization method" of a
\ref guide-write-min-flt-comp-cls "minimal filter component class",
consider implementing:

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-fini "Finalize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Destroy, free, or otherwise finalize any user data you attached to
    the component with bt_self_component_set_data() in the
    initialization method.

    Set it with bt_component_class_filter_set_finalize_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD() macro
    in a
    \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-mip "Get supported MIP versions"</dt>
  <dd>
    Implement this \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method" if
    your component class supports \bt_mip (MIP)&nbsp;1: without it,
    the library assumes that any instance of your component class only
    supports MIP&nbsp;version&nbsp;0.

    See \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1 to learn how to support both MIP
    versions.

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_filter_set_get_supported_mip_versions_method(),
    or with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-iport-connected "Input port connected"</dt>
  <dd>
    React to one of the \bt_p_iport of the component becoming
    connected, for example to add more ports depending on the \bt_comp
    at the other end of the new \bt_conn (see \ref guide-graph-listeners
    for another way to react to topology changes from outside the
    component).

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_filter_set_input_port_connected_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_INPUT_PORT_CONNECTED_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-oport-connected "Output port connected"</dt>
  <dd>
    React to one of the \bt_p_oport of the component becoming
    connected.

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_filter_set_output_port_connected_method(), or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_OUTPUT_PORT_CONNECTED_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "Query"</dt>
  <dd>
    Let users of your component class, before or without instantiating
    it, ask arbitrary questions about it.

    See \ref guide-query to learn how to implement and call this
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method".

    Set it with bt_component_class_filter_set_query_method(), or with
    the BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD() macro in a
    plugin definition file.
  </dd>
</dl>

@section guide-write-full-flt-comp-cls-msg-iter-cls-methods Message iterator class methods

On top of the mandatory
\link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method, consider
implementing:

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-init "Initialize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Perform any setup your \bt_msg_iter needs before its "next" method
    is called for the first time, and attach user data to it with
    bt_self_message_iterator_set_data().

    This is typically where a filter message iterator creates its
    upstream message iterator with
    bt_message_iterator_create_from_message_iterator(), from the
    \bt_iport the filter component reads from.

    Set it with bt_message_iterator_class_set_initialize_method(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_INITIALIZE_METHOD()
    macro in a
    \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-fini "Finalize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Destroy, free, or otherwise finalize any user data you attached to
    the message iterator in its initialization method, putting the
    reference of its upstream message iterator, if any, with
    bt_message_iterator_put_ref().

    Set it with bt_message_iterator_class_set_finalize_method(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-beg "Can seek beginning?"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-beg "Seek beginning"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-ns "Can seek ns from origin?"
  </dt>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-ns "Seek ns from origin"
  </dt>
  <dd>
    Make your message iterator able to seek its beginning or a specific
    point in time, typically by using the seeking capabilities of its
    own upstream message iterator (with
    bt_message_iterator_can_seek_beginning(),
    bt_message_iterator_seek_beginning(),
    bt_message_iterator_can_seek_ns_from_origin(), and
    bt_message_iterator_seek_ns_from_origin()).

    See \ref guide-seek-msg-iter to learn how to implement those
    methods.

    Set them with
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_beginning_methods() and
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_ns_from_origin_methods(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_BEGINNING_METHODS()
    and
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_NS_FROM_ORIGIN_METHODS()
    macros in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>
</dl>

Whether you implement all, some, or none of the methods above depends
entirely on the trace processing task your filter component class
needs to accomplish. See the existing \bt_name \bt_p_plugin
for real-world examples of filter component classes.

@page guide-write-full-sink-comp-cls Write a complete sink component class

This guide lists all the optional methods you can add to a
\ref guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls "minimal sink component class" to
make it robust and complete, ready for production use.

On top of the mandatory
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume "consuming" method, the
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-init "initialization" method, and the
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-graph-configured "\"graph is configured\""
method of a
\ref guide-write-min-sink-comp-cls "minimal sink component class",
consider implementing:

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-fini "Finalize"</dt>
  <dd>
    Destroy, free, or otherwise finalize any user data you attached to
    the component with bt_self_component_set_data() in the
    initialization method, putting the reference of the upstream
    message iterator, if any and if it's not already put, with
    bt_message_iterator_put_ref().

    Set it with bt_component_class_sink_set_finalize_method(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_FINALIZE_METHOD()
    macro in a
    \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-mip "Get supported MIP versions"</dt>
  <dd>
    Implement this \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method" if
    your component class supports \bt_mip (MIP)&nbsp;1: without it,
    the library assumes that any instance of your component class only
    supports MIP&nbsp;version&nbsp;0.

    See \ref guide-mip-0-to-mip-1 to learn how to support both MIP
    versions.

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_sink_set_get_supported_mip_versions_method(),
    or with the
    BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_GET_SUPPORTED_MIP_VERSIONS_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-iport-connected "Input port connected"</dt>
  <dd>
    React to one of the \bt_p_iport of the component becoming
    connected, for example to add more input ports (see
    \ref guide-graph-listeners for another way to react to topology
    changes from outside the component).

    Set it with
    bt_component_class_sink_set_input_port_connected_method(),
    or
    with the BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_INPUT_PORT_CONNECTED_METHOD()
    macro in a plugin definition file.
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "Query"</dt>
  <dd>
    Let users of your component class, before or without instantiating
    it, ask arbitrary questions about it.

    See \ref guide-query to learn how to implement and call this
    \ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method".

    Set it with bt_component_class_sink_set_query_method(), or with
    the BT_PLUGIN_SINK_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD() macro in a
    plugin definition file.
  </dd>
</dl>

Also consider:

- Checking whether or not your component is
  \ref guide-intr-graph "interrupted" with
  bt_self_component_sink_is_interrupted() during a long consuming
  operation, and returning
  #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN or
  #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_ERROR when it is.

- Making your \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume "consuming" method
  \ref guide-seek-msg-iter "seek" its upstream message iterator in
  reaction to some external event or initialization parameter.

- Supporting more than one \bt_iport, in which case your
  \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-graph-configured "\"graph is configured\""
  method creates one \bt_msg_iter per connected input port, and your
  consuming method gets messages from each of them, for example in
  round-robin fashion.

Whether you implement all, some, or none of the methods above depends
entirely on the trace processing task your sink component class needs
to accomplish. See the existing \bt_name \bt_p_plugin
for real-world examples of sink component classes.

@page guide-query Query an object from a component class

This guide shows both how to implement the optional
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "query" \bt_comp_cls method and how to
call it to perform a query operation, that is, to get an arbitrary
object from a \bt_comp_cls without necessarily having to instantiate
it.

A query operation is how, for example, a \bt_src_comp_cls can offer
the list of traces available at a given path, or how a component class
communicating with a distant server can offer the list of live tracing
sessions currently available on this server, without any \bt_graph
being involved.

The semantics of the parameters and of the returned object of a query
operation are completely up to the component class implementation: read
its documentation to know which objects it offers, and with which
parameters.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

@section guide-query-impl Implement the query method

Implement the \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "query"
\ref api-comp-cls-dev-class-meth "class method" of your \bt_comp_cls:

@code
static bt_component_class_query_method_status
my_query(bt_self_component_class_source * const self_comp_class,
         bt_private_query_executor * const query_exec,
         const char * const object_name, const bt_value * const params,
         void * const method_data, const bt_value ** const result)
{
    if (strcmp(object_name, "tamebsz-count") == 0) {
        *result = bt_value_integer_unsigned_create_init(23);
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }

    return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_UNKNOWN_OBJECT;
}
@endcode

Within this method:

- Compare \bt_p{object_name} to each object name your component class
  offers, and set \bt_p{*result} to a \em new \bt_val reference
  representing the result of the query operation.

- If \bt_p{object_name} isn't an object your component class offers,
  then return #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_UNKNOWN_OBJECT.

- To check the \ref api-qexec-prop-log-lvl "logging level" of the query
  executor performing the operation, first upcast \bt_p{query_exec}
  with bt_private_query_executor_as_query_executor_const(), then use
  bt_query_executor_get_logging_level().

- If your query operation can block, then periodically check whether or
  not the query executor is interrupted (see the \ref guide-intr-query
  guide) and, if it is, return
  #BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN.

Set this method with bt_component_class_source_set_query_method(),
bt_component_class_filter_set_query_method(), or
bt_component_class_sink_set_query_method(), depending on the type of
your component class.

@section guide-query-perform Perform a query operation

To call the query method of a component class:

<ol>
  <li>
    Create a \bt_qexec with bt_query_executor_create(), passing the
    component class to query, the name of the object to query, and the
    query parameters, or with
    bt_query_executor_create_with_method_data() if you also need to
    pass custom data to the query method.

    @code
    bt_query_executor * const query_exec = bt_query_executor_create(comp_class,
                                                                    "tamebsz-count",
                                                                    params);
    @endcode
  <li>
    \bt_dt_opt Set the \ref api-qexec-prop-log-lvl "logging level" of
    the query executor with bt_query_executor_set_logging_level().
  <li>
    Perform the query operation with bt_query_executor_query().

    @code
    const bt_value *result;

    const bt_query_executor_query_status status = bt_query_executor_query(query_exec, &result);
    @endcode

    If this function returns #BT_QUERY_EXECUTOR_QUERY_STATUS_AGAIN, then
    you can call it again later. If it returns
    #BT_QUERY_EXECUTOR_QUERY_STATUS_UNKNOWN_OBJECT, then the component
    class doesn't offer the requested object.
</ol>

To interrupt a running query operation, see \ref guide-intr-query.

Once you're done with the query executor and the result value, put
their references with bt_query_executor_put_ref() and
bt_value_put_ref().

@page guide-seek-msg-iter Make a message iterator seek

This guide shows both how to make an existing \bt_msg_iter seek and how
to implement the seeking methods of a \bt_msg_iter_cls so that its
message iterators support seeking.

A message iterator can support two seeking
\ref api-msg-iter-ops "operations":

<dl>
  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-op-seek-beg "Seek beginning"</dt>
  <dd>
    Make the message iterator seek the very first \bt_msg of its
    \ref api-msg-seq "sequence".
  </dd>

  <dt>\ref api-msg-iter-op-seek-ns "Seek ns from origin"</dt>
  <dd>
    Make the message iterator seek a message occurring at or after a
    specific time, expressed in nanoseconds from some
    \ref api-tir-clock-cls-origin "clock origin".

    You don't specify \em which clock class: because all the
    \bt_p_cs of the messages within the \ref api-msg-seq "sequence" of
    a given message iterator are
    \ref api-tir-clock-cls-origin "correlatable", they share a
    single, common origin, and it's in terms of this shared origin
    that you express the time to seek.
  </dd>
</dl>

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

@section guide-seek-msg-iter-make Make a message iterator seek

Before you make a message iterator seek, check whether or not it
currently can with bt_message_iterator_can_seek_beginning() or
bt_message_iterator_can_seek_ns_from_origin():

@code
bt_bool can_seek_beginning;

bt_message_iterator_can_seek_beginning(msg_iter, &can_seek_beginning);

if (can_seek_beginning) {
    bt_message_iterator_seek_beginning(msg_iter);
}
@endcode

Then make the message iterator seek with
bt_message_iterator_seek_beginning() or
bt_message_iterator_seek_ns_from_origin(), passing, for the latter, the
specific time to seek in nanoseconds from the origin.

If either seeking function fails (returns a status which isn't
#BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_SEEK_BEGINNING_STATUS_OK or
#BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_SEEK_NS_FROM_ORIGIN_STATUS_OK), you can only call
bt_message_iterator_seek_beginning() or
bt_message_iterator_seek_ns_from_origin() again afterwards: no other
message iterator operation is valid.

@section guide-seek-msg-iter-impl Implement the seeking methods of a message iterator class

To make the message iterators of your \bt_msg_iter_cls support seeking,
implement one or both of:

<dl>
  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-beg "\"Seek beginning\"" and,
    if your message iterator cannot \em always seek its beginning,
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-beg "\"can seek beginning?\""
  </dt>
  <dd>
    @code
    static bt_message_iterator_class_seek_beginning_method_status
    my_iter_seek_beginning(bt_self_message_iterator * const self_msg_iter)
    {
        struct my_iter_data * const data = bt_self_message_iterator_get_data(self_msg_iter);

        /* Reset the iteration state of `data` here */

        return BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_BEGINNING_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set both methods with
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_beginning_methods(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_BEGINNING_METHODS()
    macro in a \ref guide-create-plugin "plugin definition file".
  </dd>

  <dt>
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-ns "\"Seek ns from origin\"" and, if
    your message iterator cannot \em always seek any given time,
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-can-seek-ns "\"can seek ns from origin?\""
  </dt>
  <dd>
    @code
    static bt_message_iterator_class_seek_ns_from_origin_method_status
    my_iter_seek_ns_from_origin(bt_self_message_iterator * const self_msg_iter,
                                const int64_t ns_from_origin)
    {
        struct my_iter_data * const data = bt_self_message_iterator_get_data(self_msg_iter);

        /* Make `data` ready to emit a message at or after `ns_from_origin` here */

        return BT_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_NS_FROM_ORIGIN_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
    }
    @endcode

    Set them with
    bt_message_iterator_class_set_seek_ns_from_origin_methods(), or
    with the
    BT_PLUGIN_FILTER_COMPONENT_CLASS_MESSAGE_ITERATOR_CLASS_SEEK_NS_FROM_ORIGIN_METHODS()
    macro in a plugin definition file.

    Alternatively, if your message iterator can seek
    <em>forward</em>, that is, if all the \bt_p_msg of its sequence
    have a \bt_cs, then you don't strictly need to implement this
    method: set the message iterator as being able to seek forward with
    bt_self_message_iterator_configuration_set_can_seek_forward() within
    its \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-init "initialization method" and
    libbabeltrace2 implements bt_message_iterator_seek_ns_from_origin()
    for you, using the
    \ref api-msg-iter-cls-meth-seek-beg "seek beginning" method and
    repeated calls to the "next" method.

    @important
        This fallback implementation is inefficient, as it may need to
        seek the beginning of the message iterator sequence and then
        replay many messages to reach the requested time: implement the
        "seek ns from origin" method yourself whenever your message
        iterator can seek more efficiently.
  </dd>
</dl>

If your component class is a \bt_flt_comp_cls, then its message iterator
will typically implement seeking in terms of the seeking capabilities
of its own upstream message iterator, using
bt_message_iterator_can_seek_beginning(),
bt_message_iterator_seek_beginning(),
bt_message_iterator_can_seek_ns_from_origin(), and
bt_message_iterator_seek_ns_from_origin().

If you need to block the thread while computing whether or not your
message iterator can currently seek, or while actually seeking, you can
instead report to try again later to the caller by returning the
<code>*_STATUS_AGAIN</code> status code of the corresponding method.

@page guide-intr-graph Interrupt a running graph

This guide shows how to interrupt a running \bt_graph, that is, how to
make bt_graph_run() or bt_graph_run_once() return
#BT_GRAPH_RUN_STATUS_AGAIN (or #BT_GRAPH_RUN_ONCE_STATUS_AGAIN) as soon
as possible from another thread or from a signal handler.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

Every trace processing graph has a default \bt_intr as soon as you
create it with bt_graph_create(). To interrupt a graph, either:

<ul>
  <li>
    Borrow its default interrupter with
    bt_graph_borrow_default_interrupter() and set it with
    bt_interrupter_set():

    @code
    bt_interrupter_set(bt_graph_borrow_default_interrupter(graph));
    @endcode
  <li>
    Add your own \bt_intr with bt_graph_add_interrupter(), \em before
    you run the graph with bt_graph_run() or bt_graph_run_once(), and
    set it, also with bt_interrupter_set(), whenever you need to
    interrupt the graph:

    @code
    bt_interrupter * const interrupter = bt_interrupter_create();

    bt_graph_add_interrupter(graph, interrupter);

    /* ... */

    bt_interrupter_set(interrupter);
    @endcode

    Adding your own interrupter is useful, for example, if you need to
    share a single interrupter amongst several trace processing graphs,
    or between a graph and a \ref guide-intr-query "query executor".
</ul>

When bt_graph_run() or bt_graph_run_once() returns
#BT_GRAPH_RUN_STATUS_AGAIN (or #BT_GRAPH_RUN_ONCE_STATUS_AGAIN), check
the state of the interrupter(s) of the graph with
bt_interrupter_is_set() to distinguish between a \bt_sink_comp
returning "try again" and the graph actually getting interrupted.

Within a \bt_comp \ref api-comp-cls-dev-methods "method" or a
\bt_msg_iter_cls \ref api-msg-iter-cls-methods "method", you can check
whether or not the owning graph is interrupted with
bt_self_component_sink_is_interrupted() or
bt_self_message_iterator_is_interrupted(): a well-behaved,
long-running \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume "consuming" method or
\link api-msg-iter-cls-meth-next "next"\endlink method should check
this periodically and return an <code>*_STATUS_AGAIN</code> or
<code>*_STATUS_ERROR</code> status code, depending on its capability to
continue the current operation later, when it is.

For example, a \bt_sink_comp \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-consume
"consuming" method may check whether or not it's interrupted before
doing any actual work:

@code
static bt_component_class_sink_consume_method_status
my_sink_consume(bt_self_component_sink * const self_comp)
{
    if (bt_self_component_sink_is_interrupted(self_comp)) {
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN;
    }

    /* Do some work here */

    return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_SINK_CONSUME_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
}
@endcode

Once you get back the control of the thread after an interrupted
trace processing graph, you can reset a given \bt_intr with
bt_interrupter_reset() and, for example, call bt_graph_run() again.

@page guide-intr-query Interrupt a query operation

This guide shows how to interrupt a running
\ref guide-query "query operation", that is, how to make
bt_query_executor_query() return
#BT_QUERY_EXECUTOR_QUERY_STATUS_AGAIN as soon as possible from another
thread or from a signal handler.

@note
    To keep the following code snippets focused, they don't check the
    return status codes of API functions, but you \em must check them in
    production code.

Every \bt_qexec has a default \bt_intr as soon as you create it with
bt_query_executor_create() or
bt_query_executor_create_with_method_data(). To interrupt a query
executor, either:

<ul>
  <li>
    Borrow its default interrupter with
    bt_query_executor_borrow_default_interrupter() and set it with
    bt_interrupter_set():

    @code
    bt_interrupter_set(bt_query_executor_borrow_default_interrupter(query_exec));
    @endcode
  <li>
    Add your own \bt_intr with bt_query_executor_add_interrupter(),
    \em before you call bt_query_executor_query(), and set it, also
    with bt_interrupter_set(), whenever you need to interrupt the
    query executor:

    @code
    bt_interrupter * const interrupter = bt_interrupter_create();

    bt_query_executor_add_interrupter(query_exec, interrupter);

    /* ... */

    bt_interrupter_set(interrupter);
    @endcode

    Adding your own interrupter is useful, for example, if you need to
    share a single interrupter amongst several query executors, or
    between a query executor and a
    \ref guide-intr-graph "trace processing graph".
</ul>

A component class \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-query "query" method
should periodically check whether or not the query executor performing
the operation is interrupted with bt_query_executor_is_interrupted(),
using the private view of the query executor it receives, upcast with
bt_private_query_executor_as_query_executor_const(), and return
#BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN when it is.

For example, expanding on the \ref guide-query-impl "query method"
example above:

@code
static bt_component_class_query_method_status
my_query(bt_self_component_class_source * const self_comp_class,
         bt_private_query_executor * const query_exec,
         const char * const object_name, const bt_value * const params,
         void * const method_data, const bt_value ** const result)
{
    const bt_query_executor * const pub_query_exec =
        bt_private_query_executor_as_query_executor_const(query_exec);

    if (bt_query_executor_is_interrupted(pub_query_exec)) {
        return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_AGAIN;
    }

    /* Do some work here */

    return BT_COMPONENT_CLASS_QUERY_METHOD_STATUS_OK;
}
@endcode

Once you get back the control of the thread after an interrupted query
operation, you can reset a given \bt_intr with bt_interrupter_reset()
and, for example, call bt_query_executor_query() again.

@page guide-graph-listeners Use graph listeners to react to topology events

This guide shows how to get notified, from outside a \bt_comp, when a
\bt_comp of a \bt_graph adds a \bt_port, so that you can react to it,
for example by connecting the new port automatically.

A component can add a port:

- At initialization time, that is, when you
  \ref guide-create-graph "add" the component to the graph with one of
  the <code>bt_graph_add_*_component*()</code> functions.

- When one of its ports gets connected, that is, when you
  \ref guide-create-graph "connect" ports with
  bt_graph_connect_ports() and the component reacts within its
  \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-iport-connected "\"input port connected\""
  or
  \ref api-comp-cls-dev-meth-oport-connected "\"output port connected\""
  method.

To get notified when any component of a trace processing graph adds a
port, add one or more "port added" listeners to the graph, depending on
the component and port types you're interested in, \em before you
\ref api-graph-lc "configure" it:

<dl>
  <dt>\bt_c_src_comp \bt_oport</dt>
  <dd>bt_graph_add_source_component_output_port_added_listener()</dd>

  <dt>\bt_c_flt_comp \bt_iport</dt>
  <dd>bt_graph_add_filter_component_input_port_added_listener()</dd>

  <dt>\bt_c_flt_comp output port</dt>
  <dd>bt_graph_add_filter_component_output_port_added_listener()</dd>

  <dt>\bt_c_sink_comp input port</dt>
  <dd>bt_graph_add_sink_component_input_port_added_listener()</dd>
</dl>

For example, to get notified when a filter component adds an input
port:

@code
static bt_graph_listener_func_status
my_flt_iport_added(const bt_component_filter * const comp,
                   const bt_port_input * const port, void * const user_data)
{
    /* React to the new port here, for example connect it */
    return BT_GRAPH_LISTENER_FUNC_STATUS_OK;
}
@endcode

@code
bt_listener_id listener_id;

bt_graph_add_filter_component_input_port_added_listener(graph, my_flt_iport_added,
                                                        NULL, &listener_id);
@endcode

Within a "port added" listener function, you can
\ref api-graph-lc-add "add more components" and
\ref api-graph-lc-connect "connect more component ports", for example
to connect the newly added port to an existing or to a new component,
extending the topology of the graph dynamically as it gets configured.

The \bt_p{user_data} parameter of a listener function is whatever
pointer you pass as the corresponding parameter of the
<code>bt_graph_add_*_added_listener()</code> function.
*/
